<html><head><title>Roles &amp;amp;amp;amp; Tasks</title>
<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><img id="imagen" src="4_Participation_files/participationtrail04.jpg" width="90%" onerror="this.style.display='none'" style="display: none;"><h1 style="margin-bottom: 16px; font-family: Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.4; color: rgb(33, 37, 41); font-size: 32px;"><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><font color="#000000"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 18pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><span style="font-weight: bolder;">oles and tasks</span></span><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><span style="font-weight: bolder;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;  </span><o:p></o:p></span></font></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">What different roles and tasks exist in a transdisciplinary team? And what about you: how exactly can you participate and contribute to the project? This torch discusses three basic tasks: acting, deciding and guiding - tasks that of course overlap as well.<o:p></o:p></font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span style="font-weight: bolder;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">A</font></span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 14pt;">ct </span></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">The basic task of ‘acting’ refers to your own direct contributions, regardless of your background with the project. Do not hesitate to take responsibility, step out of your own beaten path and encourage others to do the same.<o:p></o:p></font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">Another typical attitude for transdisciplinary working is an inquisitive mindset. Since nothing is clear at the beginning of a transdisciplinary project, you must be willing to create and explore new knowledge. </font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000"><span style="font-weight: bolder;">Decision-making </span><o:p></o:p></font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">In the project, you make decisions together with different stakeholders, not just by those with the most experience or the loudest voice. In the safe environment, everyone should be heard.</font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000"><span style="font-weight: bolder;">Guide </span><o:p></o:p></font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">Although you want to work together as equally as possible, sometimes it is necessary for one individual to oversee the process, encourage breakthroughs and balance the needs of the group with the practical framework. You can call these people the ‘guides’.&nbsp;<o:p></o:p></font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">Guides have 2 roles in transdisciplinary projects. First, they often take the initiative to set up and co-design a transdisciplinary project. Later, they promote progress as coaches. This applies especially - but not exclusively! - to the teaching environment. Here, teachers or other students can guide the learning process of participating students. <o:p></o:p></font></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;"><font color="#000000">&nbsp;</font></span></p></h1><h1 style="font-size: 32px; line-height: 1.4; margin-bottom: 16px; font-family: Roboto, sans-serif;"><p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: normal;"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 14pt;">Regardless of the role you take on during the project, you also participate from a certain stakeholder position. Your previous experiences will almost certainly influence how you experience a transdisciplinary process.</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 14pt;">&nbsp;</span></p></h1></body></html>